#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
smap-uaccess-lint - FAILS THE BUILD if (A) the SMAP mechanism itself has
regressed, or (B) a NEW Ring-0 access to user memory has appeared that is
neither routed through the copy_*_user primitives, nor bracketed by
uaccess_begin()/uaccess_end(), nor recorded on kernel/smap-uaccess.manifest.

WHY THIS EXISTS (#645)
======================
With CR4.SMAP set, ANY Ring-0 read or write to a user-accessible page (U/S=1)
raises #PF unless RFLAGS.AC is set. So an unwrapped user access is no longer a
subtle TOCTOU risk that builds clean and mostly works: it is a HARD FAULT on
whatever path takes it. The failure mode is "this machine does not boot", and
the compiler cannot see it coming.

THE TWO GAPS THIS CLOSES
------------------------
#566's copy-user-lint is a good gate with two documented limits, and BOTH of
them are load-bearing under SMAP. They were measured, not assumed:

  GAP 1 - FILE SCOPE. copy-user-lint's own header says it "reasons about
  proc/syscall.c ONLY". Measured against the tree, 43 of the 122 live
  copy_*_user call sites are in OTHER files (net/socket.c, mm/fault.c,
  proc/signal.c, proc/thread.c, proc/process.c), and the heaviest unwrapped
  user accesses in the kernel are outside syscall.c entirely: the ELF loader
  writing PT_LOAD segments and relocations into the child address space, the
  initial-argv builder writing the user stack, and the whole signal-frame
  build/restore path. None of that was linted by anything. A new raw user deref
  in any of those files compiled clean.

  GAP 2 - TRACING DEPTH. copy-user-lint traces ONE level (dispatcher case ->
  handler defined in syscall.c). A user pointer handed to a SECOND-level helper
  is invisible to it. That is not hypothetical: sc_copy_str() in proc/syscall.c
  reads a user string byte-by-byte with NO validation at all, and is reached as
  case -> sys_session_unlock()/sys_auth_lockout()/sys_set_autologin() ->
  sc_copy_str(), i.e. exactly two levels, so the gate never saw it.

This lint widens BOTH: the file set is derived (not hand-listed), and the call
tracing is depth-limited but greater than one.

RULE A - MECHANISM INTEGRITY (precise, textual, zero false positives)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The SMAP bracket is only as good as four specific pieces of code. Each one, if
quietly deleted, leaves a kernel that still BOOTS and still SAYS "SMAP: ENABLED"
while the protection is partly or wholly gone. That is the worst possible
outcome, so each is asserted here:

  A1 mm/uaccess.asm      Every one of the 4 primitives must SET AC before its
                         faultable region and CLEAR AC on EVERY return path -
                         including the *_ex_fixup path, which mm/fault.c reaches
                         by rewriting RIP and which therefore SKIPS the normal
                         post-region CLAC. Miss that one and every -EFAULT
                         leaves the rest of the syscall running with SMAP off.
  A2 cpu/idt.asm         isr_common must CLAC (no handler may run with SMAP
                         disabled) and CLD (the SysV ABI requires DF=0 and an
                         IDT gate does not clear it).
  A3 proc/syscall.c      IA32_FMASK must clear AC and DF on SYSCALL entry. AC is
                         writable from CPL 3, so without the AC bit a Ring-3
                         process runs the ENTIRE syscall with SMAP disabled just
                         by setting it before the instruction.
  A4 security/security.c g_smap_active must be armed from the FEATURE BIT (which
                         required a CR4 readback), never from the mere request
                         flag. Arming it from the request would execute STAC on
                         a machine whose CR4 write was silently dropped.

RULE B - NO NEW UNWRAPPED USER ACCESS
-------------------------------------
B1 LEDGER FRESHNESS. Every manifest entry names a file, a line and a substring.
   If the substring is no longer on that line, the entry is STALE and the lint
   FAILS. A fixed site MUST be delisted. This is what stops the ledger rotting
   into a list of things that used to be true (the #514 defect class).

B2 CR3-WINDOW SCAN. A kernel routine that switches CR3 to another process's
   address space and then dereferences memory is, by construction, touching a
   foreign user mapping. These windows are textually unmistakable, so any
   memory access inside one must be AC-bracketed or manifested.

B3 VALIDATED-THEN-DEREFFED SCAN, widened. The same rule copy-user-lint applies
   inside syscall.c, applied across the DERIVED watch set: any file that calls
   validate_user_ptr / validate_user_string / mm_prefault_range / a copy_*_user
   wrapper is watched automatically, so a new file joins the moment it starts
   handling user pointers. Nothing to hand-maintain, nothing to forget.

HONEST LIMITS - READ THESE BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN RUN
------------------------------------------------------
* This is pattern analysis over C text, NOT whole-program dataflow. It finds the
  structural markers that the sites measured in this tree actually exhibit. A
  user pointer laundered through a struct field, a global, or a function pointer
  will not be traced.
* It cannot prove the ABSENCE of an unwrapped access. It can only prove that no
  NEW one matching its detectors has appeared since the ledger was written.
* GREEN here does NOT mean SMAP is safe to enable. It means no new site of the
  recognised shapes has landed. The ledger's KNOWN_GAP entries are still live
  faults waiting to happen; they are listed precisely so nobody mistakes a green
  build for a clean one.
"""

import argparse, os, re, sys, tempfile

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# helpers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def read(path):
    with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
        return fh.read()

def strip_comments(text, keep_strings=False):
    """Remove /*...*/ and //... so a rule cannot be satisfied BY A COMMENT.
    Newlines are preserved so line numbers stay exact.

    String literals are blanked too by default, so that a rule cannot be
    satisfied by a mention inside a message either. keep_strings=True is for
    RULE B2 ONLY, which must see inline-asm bodies: the CR3 write it hunts for
    lives INSIDE a string literal (`__asm__("mov %0, %%cr3" ...)`), so blanking
    strings made every foreign-CR3 window invisible and B2 silently matched
    nothing. That bug was caught by the self-test, which is the entire reason
    the self-test exists."""
    out = []
    i, n = 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        if text.startswith("/*", i):
            j = text.find("*/", i + 2)
            j = n if j < 0 else j + 2
            out.append("".join(c if c == "\n" else " " for c in text[i:j]))
            i = j
        elif text.startswith("//", i):
            j = text.find("\n", i)
            j = n if j < 0 else j
            out.append(" " * (j - i))
            i = j
        elif text[i] in "\"'":
            q = text[i]; j = i + 1
            while j < n and text[j] != q:
                j += 2 if text[j] == "\\" else 1
            j = min(j + 1, n)
            out.append(text[i:j] if keep_strings
                       else "".join(c if c == "\n" else " " for c in text[i:j]))
            i = j
        else:
            out.append(text[i]); i += 1
    return "".join(out)

def asm_strip(text):
    """NASM comments start with ';'. Blank them, keep line count."""
    return "\n".join(ln.split(";", 1)[0] for ln in text.split("\n"))


class Report:
    def __init__(self):
        self.fail = []
        self.notes = []
    def bad(self, rule, msg):
        self.fail.append((rule, msg))
    def note(self, msg):
        self.notes.append(msg)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE A - mechanism integrity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PRIMITIVES = ["copy", "set", "strncpy", "strnlen"]

def rule_a1(root, rep):
    path = os.path.join(root, "mm", "uaccess.asm")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm not found; the 4 user-copy primitives are "
                      "the ONLY place the kernel touches user memory, so its "
                      "absence means this lint is checking nothing.")
        return
    lines = asm_strip(read(path)).split("\n")

    entry = {}
    for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(__uaccess_\w+):", ln)
        if m:
            entry.setdefault(m.group(1), i)

    for p in PRIMITIVES:
        name, start_lbl = "__uaccess_" + p, "__uaccess_%s_ex_start" % p
        if name not in entry:
            rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm: primitive %s is missing entirely." % name)
            continue
        beg = entry[name]
        # routine ends where the NEXT primitive's entry label begins
        ends = [entry["__uaccess_" + q] for q in PRIMITIVES
                if "__uaccess_" + q in entry and entry["__uaccess_" + q] > beg]
        end = min(ends) if ends else len(lines)
        body = lines[beg:end]

        if start_lbl not in entry:
            rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm: %s has no %s label; the fault-fixup "
                          "exception table in mm/fault.c is built from these "
                          "symbols." % (name, start_lbl))
            continue

        # AC must be SET before the faultable region begins.
        stac_rel = [k for k, ln in enumerate(body) if re.search(r"\bSMAP_STAC\b", ln)]
        start_rel = entry[start_lbl] - beg
        if not stac_rel or min(stac_rel) >= start_rel:
            rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm: %s does not SMAP_STAC before %s. Under "
                          "SMAP its user access faults on the first byte."
                          % (name, start_lbl))

        # AC must be CLEARED on EVERY return path, fixup included.
        last_label = None
        for k, ln in enumerate(body):
            if re.match(r"^\s*[\w.]+:", ln):
                last_label = k
            if re.match(r"^\s*ret\b", ln):
                lo = last_label if last_label is not None else 0
                if not any(re.search(r"\bSMAP_CLAC\b", body[j]) for j in range(lo, k)):
                    rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm:%d: `ret` in %s is reachable "
                                  "without SMAP_CLAC. AC would stay SET after "
                                  "return, disabling SMAP for the rest of the "
                                  "syscall." % (beg + k + 1, name))
        fx = "__uaccess_%s_ex_fixup" % p
        if fx in entry:
            f_rel = entry[fx] - beg
            tail = body[f_rel:]
            cut = next((k for k, ln in enumerate(tail[1:], 1)
                        if re.match(r"^\s*[\w.]+:", ln)), len(tail))
            if not any(re.search(r"\bSMAP_CLAC\b", ln) for ln in tail[:cut]):
                rep.bad("A1", "mm/uaccess.asm: %s does not SMAP_CLAC. mm/fault.c "
                              "reaches this label by REWRITING RIP, which skips "
                              "the CLAC after the region, so every -EFAULT would "
                              "return with SMAP disabled." % fx)


def rule_a2(root, rep):
    path = os.path.join(root, "cpu", "idt.asm")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        rep.bad("A2", "cpu/idt.asm not found."); return
    txt = asm_strip(read(path))
    m = re.search(r"^isr_common:(.*?)call\s+isr_handler", txt, re.S | re.M)
    if not m:
        rep.bad("A2", "cpu/idt.asm: could not find isr_common ... call isr_handler. "
                      "That is the single entry chokepoint for every interrupt, "
                      "trap and exception; if it moved, this rule must move too.")
        return
    head = m.group(1)
    if not re.search(r"^\s*clac\b", head, re.M):
        rep.bad("A2", "cpu/idt.asm: isr_common does not CLAC before calling the C "
                      "handler. An interrupt taken during a user copy (AC=1), or "
                      "from a Ring-3 process that set AC itself, would run the "
                      "whole handler with SMAP disabled.")
    if not re.search(r"^\s*cld\b", head, re.M):
        rep.bad("A2", "cpu/idt.asm: isr_common does not CLD. x86-64 does not clear "
                      "DF on an IDT gate, the SysV ABI requires DF=0, and gcc "
                      "emits `rep movsb`. Ring 3 can `std` and wait for a timer "
                      "interrupt to run kernel string ops backwards.")


def rule_a3(root, rep):
    path = os.path.join(root, "proc", "syscall.c")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        rep.bad("A3", "proc/syscall.c not found."); return
    txt = strip_comments(read(path))
    m = re.search(r"wrmsr\s*\(\s*MSR_SFMASK\s*,([^;]*)\)", txt)
    if not m:
        rep.bad("A3", "proc/syscall.c: no wrmsr(MSR_SFMASK, ...) found. Without it "
                      "nothing clears AC or DF on SYSCALL entry.")
        return
    mask = m.group(1)
    for bit, macro, why in (
        ("AC", "SFMASK_AC", "AC is writable from CPL 3, so a Ring-3 process can set "
                            "it and run the ENTIRE syscall with SMAP disabled."),
        ("DF", "SFMASK_DF", "DF is not cleared by SYSCALL; gcc's `rep movsb` then "
                            "runs backwards inside the kernel."),
    ):
        if macro not in mask:
            rep.bad("A3", "proc/syscall.c: IA32_FMASK does not include %s. %s"
                          % (macro, why))
    for macro, val in (("SFMASK_AC", 0x40000), ("SFMASK_DF", 0x400)):
        d = re.search(r"#define\s+%s\s+(0x[0-9A-Fa-f]+|\d+)" % macro, txt)
        if d and int(d.group(1), 0) != val:
            rep.bad("A3", "proc/syscall.c: %s is defined as %s, expected 0x%x."
                          % (macro, d.group(1), val))


def rule_a4(root, rep):
    path = os.path.join(root, "security", "security.c")
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        rep.bad("A4", "security/security.c not found."); return
    txt = strip_comments(read(path))
    asg = re.findall(r"g_smap_active\s*=\s*([^;]+);", txt)
    if not asg:
        rep.bad("A4", "security/security.c: g_smap_active is never assigned, so "
                      "every STAC/CLAC in the kernel is permanently inert while "
                      "the boot log may still claim SMAP is on.")
        return
    armed = [a for a in asg if a.strip() not in ("0", "0u")]
    if not armed:
        rep.bad("A4", "security/security.c: g_smap_active is only ever set to 0.")
        return
    for a in armed:
        if "SECURITY_FEATURE_SMAP" not in a:
            rep.bad("A4", "security/security.c: g_smap_active armed from `%s`, not "
                          "from the SECURITY_FEATURE_SMAP bit. That bit is the only "
                          "value gated on a CR4 READBACK; arming from the request "
                          "flag would execute STAC on a hypervisor that silently "
                          "dropped the CR4 write." % a.strip())


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE B - no new unwrapped user access
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

COPY_WRAPPERS = ("copy_from_user", "copy_to_user", "strncpy_from_user",
                 "strnlen_user", "clear_user")
VALIDATORS = ("validate_user_ptr", "validate_user_string", "mm_prefault_range")

def derive_watch_set(root):
    """Any .c file that validates or copies a user pointer is watched. Derived,
    never hand-listed, so a new file joins the moment it handles user memory."""
    watch = []
    needles = COPY_WRAPPERS + VALIDATORS
    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".c"):
                continue
            p = os.path.join(dirpath, f)
            rel = os.path.relpath(p, root)
            if rel.startswith("security/validate") or rel.startswith("tools"):
                continue
            body = read(p)
            if any(nd in body for nd in needles):
                watch.append(rel)
    return watch


CR3_WRITE = re.compile(r'(?:mov|movq)\s+%0\s*,\s*%%cr3|%%cr3["\s]*:\s*:\s*"r"')
CR3_ANY   = re.compile(r'%%cr3')
# B2's deref set deliberately EXCLUDES plain `p->field`. Inside a foreign-CR3
# window the KERNEL half of the address space is still mapped (that is what makes
# the window usable at all), so `prev->cr3` and friends are ordinary kernel
# struct accesses and flagging them is pure noise. What is actually dangerous
# there is an explicit cast to a foreign address, or a bulk copy: those are the
# shapes every measured site in this tree takes.
DEREF = re.compile(r'\*\s*\(\s*volatile'
                   r'|\*\s*\(\s*\w+[\w\s*]*\*\s*\)'
                   r'|\b(?:memcpy|memmove|memset|strcpy|strncpy)\s*\(')
BRACKET = re.compile(r'\buaccess_begin\s*\(|\buaccess_end\s*\(')

def rule_b2(root, watch, manifest, rep):
    """CR3-window scan: a routine that loads another address space's CR3 and then
    dereferences memory is touching a foreign user mapping by construction."""
    for rel in watch + ["exec/elf.c", "proc/process.c"]:
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        # keep_strings: the CR3 write lives inside an inline-asm STRING literal.
        lines = strip_comments(read(path), keep_strings=True).split("\n")
        if not any(CR3_ANY.search(ln) for ln in lines):
            continue
        # A window opens at a CR3 write and closes at the next CR3 write
        # (the restore). Scan the span between them.
        open_at = None
        for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
            if CR3_ANY.search(ln) and re.search(r'"r"\s*\(', ln):
                if open_at is None:
                    open_at = i
                    continue
                span = lines[open_at + 1:i]
                bracketed = any(BRACKET.search(s) for s in span)
                for k, s in enumerate(span):
                    if not DEREF.search(s):
                        continue
                    lineno = open_at + 2 + k
                    if bracketed or manifest.covers(rel, lineno, s):
                        continue
                    rep.bad("B2", "%s:%d: memory access inside a foreign-CR3 window "
                                  "is a Ring-0 touch of another process's USER "
                                  "mapping, and is neither uaccess_begin()/"
                                  "uaccess_end() bracketed nor on the manifest:\n"
                                  "        %s" % (rel, lineno, s.strip()[:110]))
                open_at = None


# #blitguard: the parameter class MUST allow balanced parens.
# It used to be `[^;{)]*`, which cannot contain a ')', so ANY function whose
# parameter list carries an attribute was never matched and therefore never
# analysed by any B rule. MEASURED on proc/syscall.c: widening this made
# EXACTLY ONE function newly visible, and it was sys_win_blit() - the one
# holding a live Ring-0 read of a Ring-3 pointer. Nothing else changed, in
# either direction. A parser that silently skips a function is worse than one
# that fails, because the skip reads as a clean result.
FUNC_RE = re.compile(r'^[A-Za-z_][\w \t*]*\b(\w+)\s*'
                     r'\(((?:[^;{()]|\((?:[^;{()]|\([^;{()]*\))*\))*)\)\s*\{', re.M)

def split_functions(text):
    """Crude but adequate brace matcher: returns [(name, params, start_line, body)]."""
    out = []
    for m in FUNC_RE.finditer(text):
        name, params = m.group(1), m.group(2)
        i = text.index("{", m.start()); depth, j = 0, i
        while j < len(text):
            if text[j] == "{": depth += 1
            elif text[j] == "}":
                depth -= 1
                if depth == 0: break
            j += 1
        out.append((name, params, text[:i].count("\n") + 1, text[i:j + 1]))
    return out


def rule_b3(root, watch, manifest, rep):
    """Validated-then-dereffed, across the whole derived watch set (GAP 1), and
    following calls into same-file helpers (GAP 2)."""
    for rel in watch:
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        text = strip_comments(read(path))
        funcs = split_functions(text)
        by_name = {f[0]: f for f in funcs}

        for name, params, startline, body in funcs:
            # user pointers = identifiers handed to a validator or to the USER
            # side of a copy wrapper, within this function.
            users = set()
            # PROVENANCE BY SOURCE, not just by validation. The signal path never
            # validates: proc/signal.c builds the sigframe by casting the Ring-3
            # stack pointer (`sigframe_t *frame = (sigframe_t *)user_rsp;`) and
            # writing ~40 fields through it, and sys_rt_sigreturn reads them back
            # the same way. Those are Ring-0 accesses to a user page on EVERY
            # signal delivery, and a validation-only rule cannot see them.
            # Likewise clear_child_tid, which is an address Ring 3 chose.
            for m in re.finditer(r"\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*=\s*[^;]*\b"
                                 r"(?:user_rsp|clear_child_tid)\b", body):
                users.add(m.group(1))
            for v in VALIDATORS:
                for m in re.finditer(r"\b%s\s*\(\s*\(?[^,()]*?\)?\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)" % v, body):
                    users.add(m.group(1))
            # #120: NAME THE ARGUMENT, do not capture a fragment of it.
            #
            # These three seeds used to read `\(?[^,()]*\)?\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)`.
            # `[^,()]*` is GREEDY, so on `copy_to_user(ubuf, ...)` it consumed
            # "ubu" and the capture group took what was left: the single letter
            # `f`. MEASURED, not theorised - `copy_from_user(&kbuf, uptr, n)`
            # seeded `r`. So the B3 scan has never tracked a copy_*_user buffer
            # BY NAME anywhere in this kernel: it tracked trailing letters, and
            # a later deref of the real pointer went unseen. It also invented
            # names, which is how it fired on #120's `file_t *f` - a kernel
            # pointer that merely shares its spelling with the phantom.
            #
            # Making it LAZY (which is what the VALIDATORS seed above already
            # does, correctly) fixes the bare case and breaks the CAST case:
            # `copy_to_user((void *)arg2, ...)` then captures `void`. Neither
            # form is right, so neither is used. The argument text is captured
            # whole and the LAST identifier in it is taken, which is the name in
            # every shape that occurs: `ubuf`, `&st` -> st, `(void *)arg2` ->
            # arg2, `(char *)f->buf` -> buf.
            def _seed_arg(argtext):
                ids = re.findall(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*", argtext or "")
                if ids:
                    users.add(ids[-1])
            for m in re.finditer(r"\bcopy_from_user\s*\([^,]+,([^,]*)", body):
                _seed_arg(m.group(1))
            for m in re.finditer(r"\b(?:copy_to_user|clear_user)\s*\(([^,]*)", body):
                _seed_arg(m.group(1))
            for m in re.finditer(r"\b(?:strncpy_from_user)\s*\([^,]+,([^,]*)", body):
                _seed_arg(m.group(1))
            if not users:
                continue
            # simple aliases: T *v = (T*)u;
            for m in re.finditer(r"\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*=\s*\(?[^;=]*?\)?\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*;", body):
                if m.group(2) in users:
                    users.add(m.group(1))

            # GAP 2: follow same-file helpers called with a user pointer, so a
            # deref two (or three) levels down is still attributed here.
            analysis = [(rel, startline, body)]
            seen, frontier, depth = {name}, [(name, body)], 0
            while frontier and depth < 3:
                nxt = []
                for _fn, fb in frontier:
                    for cm in re.finditer(r"\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*\(([^;)]*)\)", fb):
                        callee, args = cm.group(1), cm.group(2)
                        if callee in seen or callee not in by_name:
                            continue
                        if not any(re.search(r"\b%s\b" % re.escape(u), args) for u in users):
                            continue
                        seen.add(callee)
                        cn, cp, cl, cb = by_name[callee]
                        for pm in re.finditer(r"([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*(?:\[\s*\])?\s*(?:,|$)", cp):
                            users.add(pm.group(1))
                        analysis.append((rel, cl, cb))
                        nxt.append((cn, cb))
                frontier = nxt; depth += 1

            for arel, abase, abody in analysis:
                if BRACKET.search(abody):
                    continue
                # #120: an `extern` DECLARATION is not a dereference. `const
                # char *body,` in a prototype matches the same `\*\s*name`
                # shape as a real deref, and this tree declares its externs
                # INSIDE the function that uses them, so the prototypes sit in
                # the scanned body. Blanked (newlines kept, so line numbers stay
                # exact) rather than special-cased at the match, because the
                # declaration can span lines and the continuation line carries
                # no `extern` keyword of its own to test for.
                abody = re.sub(r"\bextern\b[^;{}]*;",
                               lambda m: re.sub(r"[^\n]", " ", m.group(0)), abody)
                for off, ln in enumerate(abody.split("\n")):
                    hit = None
                    for u in users:
                        u_re = re.escape(u)
                        if re.search(r"\*\s*%s\b" % u_re, ln) or \
                           re.search(r"\b%s\s*->" % u_re, ln) or \
                           re.search(r"\b%s\s*\[" % u_re, ln) or \
                           re.search(r"\b(?:memcpy|memmove|memset|strcpy|strncpy|sprintf|snprintf)\s*\([^;]*\b%s\b" % u_re, ln):
                            hit = u; break
                    if not hit:
                        continue
                    if re.search(r"\b(?:%s)\s*\(" % "|".join(COPY_WRAPPERS + VALIDATORS), ln):
                        continue
                    lineno = abase + off
                    if manifest.covers(arel, lineno, ln):
                        continue
                    rep.bad("B3", "%s:%d: Ring-0 deref of user pointer `%s` outside "
                                  "copy_*_user and outside an AC bracket:\n        %s"
                                  % (arel, lineno, hit, ln.strip()[:110]))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE B4 - A DECLARED USER BUFFER, DEREFERENCED WITHOUT A COPY PRIMITIVE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS RULE EXISTS, AND WHY B3 COULD NOT HAVE FOUND IT (#blitguard)
#
# B3 decides what a "user pointer" is by watching where the SAFETY PRIMITIVES
# are used: a name seeds its tainted set only by being handed to a validator or
# to a copy_*_user wrapper. That makes B3 a lint on the DISCIPLINE OF CODE THAT
# ALREADY USES A PRIMITIVE. It is structurally incapable of seeing a handler
# that uses no primitive at all, because such a handler seeds nothing, and B3's
# very first act is `if not users: continue`.
#
# That is not a hypothetical hole. sys_win_blit() read a Ring-3 pointer with a
# raw per-pixel dereference for its entire life, called no validator and no copy
# wrapper, and was therefore invisible to B3 while every lint reported green.
# It was ALSO invisible to tools/syscall-ptr-lint, which asks a different
# question ("does this syscall have an argtab descriptor?") and correctly
# answered yes. Three green lints, one live arbitrary-kernel-read.
#
# The fix is to seed from something that does not depend on the handler behaving:
# rustkern/argtab.rs ALREADY declares, per syscall number and per argument index,
# exactly which arguments are Ring-3 pointers and how many bytes each covers.
# That table is maintained, machine-readable, and true whether or not the handler
# is correct. So B4 asks the one question the others do not:
#
#     For every argument the argtab DECLARES to be a user buffer, does the
#     handler reach it through a copy primitive - or does it just index it?
#
# SCOPE, deliberately narrow, because a noisy lint gets disabled:
#   * BUFFERS only (Kind::R / Kind::W). Kind::Str is excluded: Ring-3 strings
#     handed raw to the filesystem are ALREADY the single KNOWN_GAP entry on
#     smap-uaccess.manifest, and re-reporting that class here would duplicate a
#     ledger rather than find anything.
#   * INDEXING only (`p[i]`). A bare `*p` also matches a pointer DECLARATION
#     (`const uint32_t *urow = ...`), and reporting the declaration you just
#     read is how a rule earns its reputation for noise.
#   * `extern ...;` statements are stripped from the body first, for the same
#     reason: a local prototype's `void *out` is a parameter star, not a deref.
#   * A copy primitive naming ANY ALIAS of the parameter counts as safe, not
#     just the parameter itself: computing a row pointer and copying THAT is
#     exactly the correct shape.
#
# MEASURED against this tree when it was written: 1 finding on the pre-fix tree
# (SYS_WIN_BLIT, param src_buffer, via src_row, at the line that page-faulted in
# the live repro), 0 findings after the fix, and 0 false positives across all 99
# argtab-declared buffer syscalls.

B4_SAFE = (r"copy_from_user|copy_to_user|clear_user|strncpy_from_user"
           r"|strnlen_user|sc_bounce_str|sc_path_from_user")


def _split_top(s):
    """Split a comma list at paren depth 0."""
    out, d, cur = [], 0, ""
    for ch in s:
        if ch in "([{":
            d += 1
        elif ch in ")]}":
            d -= 1
        if ch == "," and d == 0:
            out.append(cur.strip()); cur = ""
        else:
            cur += ch
    if cur.strip():
        out.append(cur.strip())
    return out


def rule_b4(root, manifest, rep):
    ap = os.path.join(root, "rustkern", "argtab.rs")
    hp = os.path.join(root, "proc", "syscall.h")
    cp = os.path.join(root, "proc", "syscall.c")
    if not (os.path.isfile(ap) and os.path.isfile(hp) and os.path.isfile(cp)):
        return   # not a full kernel tree (the synthetic self-test fixture)

    # 1. argtab: which (syscall number, 1-based arg index) is a user BUFFER.
    declared = {}
    for m in re.finditer(r"Desc\s*\{\s*num:\s*(\d+)\s*,\s*args:\s*\[(.*?)\]\s*,?\s*\}",
                         strip_comments(read(ap)), re.S):
        idxs = set()
        for i, it in enumerate(_split_top(m.group(2))):
            t = it.strip()
            if t == "NONE":
                continue
            core = t[3:-1].strip() if (t.startswith("sx(") and t.endswith(")")) else t
            if core.startswith("s("):
                continue          # Kind::Str: the ledgered class, see above
            idxs.add(i + 1)
        if idxs:
            declared[int(m.group(1))] = idxs

    # 2. syscall number -> SYS_* name
    name_of = {}
    for m in re.finditer(r"^#define\s+(SYS_[A-Z0-9_]+)\s+(\d+)", read(hp), re.M):
        name_of.setdefault(int(m.group(2)), m.group(1))

    # 3. dispatcher: SYS_* -> handler, and which parameter receives argN
    sc = strip_comments(read(cp))
    funcs = {f[0]: f for f in split_functions(sc)}

    for num, idxs in sorted(declared.items()):
        sysname = name_of.get(num)
        if not sysname:
            continue
        cm = re.search(r"case\s+%s\s*:(.{0,900}?)(?=\n\s*case\s+SYS_|\n\s*default\s*:)"
                       % sysname, sc, re.S)
        if not cm:
            continue
        call = re.search(r"\b(sys_\w+)\s*\(([^;]*)\)\s*;", cm.group(1))
        if not call or call.group(1) not in funcs:
            continue
        handler = call.group(1)
        callargs = _split_top(call.group(2))
        _n, params, startline, body = funcs[handler]
        body = re.sub(r"\bextern\b[^;]*;", "", body)
        pnames = []
        for p in _split_top(params):
            ids = re.findall(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*", p)
            pnames.append(ids[-1] if ids else None)

        for ai in idxs:
            pos = None
            for k, ca in enumerate(callargs):
                if re.search(r"\barg%d\b" % ai, ca):
                    pos = k; break
            if pos is None or pos >= len(pnames) or not pnames[pos]:
                continue
            pn = pnames[pos]
            alias = {pn}
            for _ in range(2):
                for am in re.finditer(r"\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*=\s*([^;=][^;]*);", body):
                    rhs = am.group(2)
                    bare = rhs
                    for cast in ("(void", "(char", "(uint32_t", "(uint8_t", "(const"):
                        bare = bare.replace(cast, "")
                    if "(" in rhs and re.search(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*\s*\(", bare):
                        continue   # RHS calls something: that is a kernel bounce
                    if any(re.search(r"\b%s\b" % re.escape(a), rhs) for a in alias):
                        alias.add(am.group(1))
            safe = any(re.search(r"\b(?:%s)\s*\([^;]*\b%s\b" % (B4_SAFE, re.escape(a)), body)
                       for a in alias)
            if safe:
                continue
            for a in sorted(alias):
                dm = re.search(r"\b%s\s*\[" % re.escape(a), body)
                if not dm:
                    continue
                lineno = startline + body[:dm.start()].count("\n")
                frag = body[dm.start():].split("\n")[0].strip()
                if manifest.covers("proc/syscall.c", lineno, frag):
                    break
                rep.bad("B4",
                        "proc/syscall.c:%d %s(): rustkern/argtab.rs declares "
                        "%s arg%d (`%s`) a USER BUFFER, and the handler indexes "
                        "it directly as `%s[...]` with no copy_*_user. That is a "
                        "Ring-0 dereference of a Ring-3-controlled address: a "
                        "#PF under SMAP, and before SMAP an unbounded read of "
                        "whatever Ring 3 named. Bounce it with copy_from_user() "
                        "one chunk at a time; sys_win_draw_image() in this "
                        "file is the reference shape."
                        % (lineno, handler, sysname, ai, pn, a))
                break


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# manifest
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Manifest:
    """Lines are:  <file>:<line>: <substring that must still be present>
    Sections [EXEMPT] (safe for a stated reason) and [KNOWN_GAP] (a real
    unwrapped access, still a live fault under SMAP, deliberately ledgered)."""
    def __init__(self, path=None):
        self.entries, self.gaps, self.path = [], [], path
        if not path or not os.path.isfile(path):
            return
        section = None
        for raw in read(path).split("\n"):
            ln = raw.strip()
            if not ln or ln.startswith("#"):
                continue
            if ln.startswith("[") and ln.endswith("]"):
                section = ln.strip("[]"); continue
            m = re.match(r"^([\w./\-]+):(\d+):\s*(.+)$", ln)
            if not m:
                continue
            e = (m.group(1), int(m.group(2)), m.group(3).strip(), section)
            self.entries.append(e)
            if section == "KNOWN_GAP":
                self.gaps.append(e)

    def covers(self, rel, lineno, text):
        for f, n, sub, _s in self.entries:
            if f == rel and abs(n - lineno) <= 2 and sub[:24] in text:
                return True
            if f == rel and sub[:24] in text:
                return True
        return False

    def check_fresh(self, root, rep):
        """B1: a manifest entry whose code no longer matches is STALE and fails."""
        for f, n, sub, _s in self.entries:
            p = os.path.join(root, f)
            if not os.path.isfile(p):
                rep.bad("B1", "manifest: %s:%d refers to a file that no longer "
                              "exists. Delete the entry." % (f, n))
                continue
            lines = read(p).split("\n")
            window = lines[max(0, n - 4): n + 3]
            if not any(sub[:24] in w for w in window):
                rep.bad("B1", "manifest: %s:%d no longer contains %r. Either the "
                              "site moved (fix the line number) or it was FIXED "
                              "(delete the entry). A ledger of things that used to "
                              "be true is how #514 happened." % (f, n, sub[:40]))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# self-test
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GOOD_UACCESS = """
section .text
%macro SMAP_STAC 0
    cmp byte [rel g_smap_active], 0
    je %%s
    stac
%%s:
%endmacro
%macro SMAP_CLAC 0
    cmp byte [rel g_smap_active], 0
    je %%c
    clac
%%c:
%endmacro
__uaccess_copy:
    mov rcx, rdx
    SMAP_STAC
__uaccess_copy_ex_start:
    rep movsb
__uaccess_copy_ex_end:
    SMAP_CLAC
    xor rax, rax
    ret
__uaccess_copy_ex_fixup:
    mov rax, rcx
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
__uaccess_set:
    mov rcx, rdx
    SMAP_STAC
__uaccess_set_ex_start:
    rep stosb
__uaccess_set_ex_end:
    SMAP_CLAC
    xor rax, rax
    ret
__uaccess_set_ex_fixup:
    mov rax, rcx
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
__uaccess_strncpy:
    SMAP_STAC
__uaccess_strncpy_ex_start:
    mov al, [rsi]
__uaccess_strncpy_ex_end:
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
__uaccess_strncpy_ex_fixup:
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
__uaccess_strnlen:
    SMAP_STAC
__uaccess_strnlen_ex_start:
    mov al, [rdi]
__uaccess_strnlen_ex_end:
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
__uaccess_strnlen_ex_fixup:
    SMAP_CLAC
    ret
"""

GOOD_IDT = """
isr_common:
    cld
    cmp byte [rel g_smap_active], 0
    je .si
    clac
.si:
    push rax
    mov rdi, rsp
    call isr_handler
    iretq
"""

GOOD_SYSCALL = """
#define MSR_SFMASK 0xC0000084
#define SFMASK_IF 0x200
#define SFMASK_TF 0x100
#define SFMASK_DF 0x400
#define SFMASK_AC 0x40000
void syscall_init(void) {
    wrmsr(MSR_SFMASK, SFMASK_IF | SFMASK_TF | SFMASK_DF | SFMASK_AC);
}
"""

GOOD_SECURITY = """
volatile uint8_t g_smap_active = 0;
void security_init(void) {
    g_smap_active = (rep.features & SECURITY_FEATURE_SMAP) ? 1u : 0u;
}
"""

GOOD_WATCHED = """
int handler(void *ubuf, unsigned n) {
    char k[64];
    if (validate_user_ptr(ubuf, n, ACCESS_READ) != VALIDATE_OK) return -1;
    if (copy_from_user(k, ubuf, n) != 0) return -1;
    return k[0];
}
"""

def build_tree(base, uaccess, idt, syscall, security, watched):
    for sub in ("mm", "cpu", "proc", "security", "net"):
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(base, sub), exist_ok=True)
    open(os.path.join(base, "mm", "uaccess.asm"), "w").write(uaccess)
    open(os.path.join(base, "cpu", "idt.asm"), "w").write(idt)
    open(os.path.join(base, "proc", "syscall.c"), "w").write(syscall)
    open(os.path.join(base, "security", "security.c"), "w").write(security)
    open(os.path.join(base, "net", "socket.c"), "w").write(watched)

def run_all(root, manifest):
    rep = Report()
    rule_a1(root, rep); rule_a2(root, rep); rule_a3(root, rep); rule_a4(root, rep)
    manifest.check_fresh(root, rep)
    watch = derive_watch_set(root)
    rule_b2(root, watch, manifest, rep)
    rule_b3(root, watch, manifest, rep)
    rule_b4(root, manifest, rep)
    return rep, watch

def self_test():
    cases = []
    def case(desc, rule, **kw):
        cases.append((desc, rule, kw))

    case("baseline: correct tree", None)
    case("A1 STAC removed from __uaccess_copy", "A1",
         uaccess=GOOD_UACCESS.replace("    mov rcx, rdx\n    SMAP_STAC\n",
                                      "    mov rcx, rdx\n", 1))
    case("A1 fixup CLAC removed (the -EFAULT leak)", "A1",
         uaccess=GOOD_UACCESS.replace("__uaccess_copy_ex_fixup:\n    mov rax, rcx\n    SMAP_CLAC\n",
                                      "__uaccess_copy_ex_fixup:\n    mov rax, rcx\n", 1))
    case("A2 isr_common CLAC removed", "A2", idt=GOOD_IDT.replace("    clac\n", ""))
    case("A2 isr_common CLD removed", "A2", idt=GOOD_IDT.replace("    cld\n", ""))
    case("A3 IA32_FMASK loses the AC bit", "A3",
         syscall=GOOD_SYSCALL.replace(" | SFMASK_AC", ""))
    case("A3 SFMASK_AC defined to the wrong bit", "A3",
         syscall=GOOD_SYSCALL.replace("#define SFMASK_AC 0x40000",
                                      "#define SFMASK_AC 0x4000"))
    case("A4 g_smap_active armed from the request flag", "A4",
         security=GOOD_SECURITY.replace("(rep.features & SECURITY_FEATURE_SMAP) ? 1u : 0u",
                                        "g_smap_requested ? 1u : 0u"))
    case("B3 new raw user deref in a watched file (GAP 1)", "B3",
         watched=GOOD_WATCHED.replace("if (copy_from_user(k, ubuf, n) != 0) return -1;",
                                      "memcpy(k, ubuf, n);"))
    case("B3 raw deref two levels down a helper (GAP 2)", "B3",
         watched="""
static void helper(const char *s, char *d) { d[0] = s[0]; }
int handler(void *ubuf, unsigned n) {
    char k[64];
    if (validate_user_ptr(ubuf, n, ACCESS_READ) != VALIDATE_OK) return -1;
    helper(ubuf, k);
    return k[0];
}
""")
    # NOTE the fixture deliberately validates a DIFFERENT pointer than the one it
    # derefs, so B3 cannot fire and only B2 can catch it. That isolation is the
    # point: it proves the CR3-window rule works on its own, rather than being
    # masked by the validated-then-dereffed rule that happens to cover the same
    # line in the real tree.
    # #120 REGRESSION. Both B3 cases above seed the user set through
    # validate_user_ptr, whose regex was always correct. NOTHING exercised the
    # copy_from_user / copy_to_user / strncpy_from_user seeds, and all three
    # were broken: a greedy `[^,()]*` swallowed the identifier and the capture
    # group took the letters left over, so `copy_to_user(ubuf, ...)` seeded the
    # name `f` and `copy_from_user(&k, uptr, n)` seeded `r`. The scan then
    # watched names that do not exist and never watched the real buffer. This
    # fixture is seeded ONLY by copy_to_user, so it was GREEN (a silent miss)
    # before the fix and is RED after it.
    case("B3 copy_to_user-seeded deref (#120 seed-regex regression)", "B3",
         watched="""
int handler(void *ubuf, unsigned n) {
    char k[64];
    k[0] = 1;
    if (copy_to_user(ubuf, k, n) != 0) return -1;
    return ubuf[0];
}
""")
    # #120: and the mirror - a PROTOTYPE naming a watched pointer must not be
    # read as a deref of it. This was GREEN only because the seed never named a
    # real pointer; with the seed fixed it goes RED unless declarations are
    # excluded.
    case("B3 extern prototype is not a deref (#120)", None,
         watched="""
int handler(void *ubuf, unsigned n) {
    extern int helper_fn(const char *ubuf, unsigned n);
    char k[64];
    if (copy_from_user(k, ubuf, n) != 0) return -1;
    return k[0];
}
""")
    case("B2 unbracketed access inside a foreign-CR3 window", "B2",
         watched="""
int loader(unsigned long pml4, char *dst, const char *src, unsigned n, void *other) {
    if (validate_user_ptr(other, 4, ACCESS_READ) != VALIDATE_OK) return -1;
    __asm__ volatile("mov %0, %%cr3" : : "r"(pml4) : "memory");
    memcpy((void *)dst, src, n);
    __asm__ volatile("mov %0, %%cr3" : : "r"(pml4) : "memory");
    return 0;
}
""")

    passed = 0
    print("smap-uaccess-lint self-test")
    print("=" * 72)
    for desc, want, kw in cases:
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:
            build_tree(td,
                       kw.get("uaccess", GOOD_UACCESS),
                       kw.get("idt", GOOD_IDT),
                       kw.get("syscall", GOOD_SYSCALL),
                       kw.get("security", GOOD_SECURITY),
                       kw.get("watched", GOOD_WATCHED))
            rep, _ = run_all(td, Manifest())
            rules = {r for r, _m in rep.fail}
            if want is None:
                ok = not rep.fail
                got = "GREEN" if ok else "RED(%s)" % ",".join(sorted(rules))
                want_s = "GREEN"
            else:
                ok = want in rules
                got = "RED(%s)" % ",".join(sorted(rules)) if rep.fail else "GREEN"
                want_s = "RED(%s)" % want
            passed += ok
            print("  [%s] %-52s want %-12s got %s"
                  % ("PASS" if ok else "FAIL", desc, want_s, got))
    print("=" * 72)
    print("%d/%d self-test cases passed" % (passed, len(cases)))
    if passed != len(cases):
        print("\nTHE GATE IS BROKEN. A lint that cannot be shown to go RED on a\n"
              "synthetic violation is not a gate. Fix it before trusting a build.")
        return 1
    print("Proven: RED on each synthetic violation, GREEN on correct code.")
    return 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="#645 SMAP user-access lint")
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=".", help="kernel/ directory")
    ap.add_argument("--manifest", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--self-test", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--smap-default", choices=("on", "off"), default=None,
                    help="the kernel's COMPILE-TIME SMAP default for this build "
                         "(off == built with -DCONFIG_NO_SMAP). With any "
                         "KNOWN_GAP entry on the ledger, `on` FAILS the build.")
    ap.add_argument("--list-watch", action="store_true")
    a = ap.parse_args()

    if a.self_test:
        return self_test()

    root = os.path.abspath(a.root)
    man = Manifest(a.manifest)
    rep, watch = run_all(root, man)

    if a.list_watch:
        print("derived watch set (%d files):" % len(watch))
        for w in watch:
            print("   ", w)
        return 0

    if man.gaps:
        print("[smap-uaccess-lint] KNOWN_GAP ledger: %d unwrapped user access(es) "
              "still live." % len(man.gaps))
        for f, n, sub, _s in man.gaps:
            print("    %s:%d  %s" % (f, n, sub[:88]))
        print("    These are REAL faults under SMAP, not theoretical. They are "
              "listed so a green build is never mistaken for a clean one.")

    # THE INTERLOCK. Shipping a kernel whose SMAP default is ON while unwrapped
    # user accesses remain does not harden anything: it #PFs on the first
    # process launch. This makes "do not turn it on yet" a build failure rather
    # than a note in a document that the next person will not read.
    if a.smap_default == "on" and man.gaps:
        rep.bad("INTERLOCK",
                "SMAP compile-time default is ON, but %d KNOWN_GAP unwrapped "
                "user access(es) remain on the ledger. Every one of them is a "
                "#PF the moment CR4.SMAP is set, on paths the kernel takes "
                "during normal operation (process launch, signal delivery, "
                "read()). Either drain the ledger or build with -DCONFIG_NO_SMAP."
                % len(man.gaps))

    if rep.fail:
        print("\n[smap-uaccess-lint] FAILED: %d violation(s)\n" % len(rep.fail))
        for rule, msg in rep.fail:
            print("  (%s) %s\n" % (rule, msg))
        print("A new unwrapped Ring-0 access to user memory FAULTS under SMAP.")
        return 1

    print("[smap-uaccess-lint] OK: SMAP mechanism intact (A1-A4); no new "
          "unwrapped user access in %d watched file(s)." % len(watch))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
